{"name":"Fast as the Wind","hex":"#c7cbc8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Firmly or securely fixed in place; stable. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Firm against attack; fortified by nature or art; impregnable; strong."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A train that calls at only some stations it passes between its origin and destination, typically just the principal stations."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fast"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"As the Wind","blocks":[{"text":"As the Wind is a live album by saxophonist Evan Parker on which he is joined by percussionists Toma Gouband, playing lithophones, and Mark Nauseef, playing metallophones. It was recorded on September 22, 2012, at St Peters in Whitstable, England, and was issued on CD in 2016 by Psi Records as the label's final release."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/05/Evan_Parker_As_the_Wind.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/As_the_Wind","wikidata":"Q127599723"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface analysis of the Great Blizzard of 1888. Areas with greater isobaric packing indicate higher winds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/1280px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cup-type anemometer on a remote meteorological station","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnemometer_2745.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Wind_barbs.gif/330px-Wind_barbs.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://t